1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Hi! In this video, we'll look at how to use BurpSuite to evaluate inputs.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Once you have discovered functionality that's worth investigating further,

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,000
you can use a range of burp tools to evaluate the user controllable inputs,

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
and determine which inputs are most important to the application's function.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
For example, do they impact the session or the state of the application?

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Using BurpSuite, you can manually evaluate individual inputs, scan the inputs, or fuzz the inputs.

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
To follow along, you can use the lab SQL injection in WHERE clause,

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
allowing retrieval of hidden data in our Web Security Academy.

9
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Before you start this tutorial, we recommend completing the following steps.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Set the test scope, map the target application,

11
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
and identify requests that you want to investigate further.

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
If you need help with any of these steps, we have separate tutorials for each of them.

13
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
First of all, we'll look at manually evaluating individual inputs.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,000
To do this, send a request to BurpRepeater.

15
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Go to the Repeater tab, and modify each input in turn.

16
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:24,000
You might want to remove the input completely, give the input an empty value,

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
or maybe insert a collaborator payload in the input by highlighting it,

18
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:36,000
and using right-click and selecting Insert collaborator payload.

19
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Once you've made your changes, click Send.

20
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Review the responses for any interesting behaviour, such as input reflections or differences in response times.

21
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
To identify subtle changes between responses, you can send the responses to BurpComparer.

22
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
In BurpComparer, you can select the two responses, click Words or Bytes to compare them,

23
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
and a new window will open with the results.

24
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Compare our highlights any differences between the two responses.

25
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Next, if you're using BurpSuite Professional, you can scan inputs to identify potential vulnerabilities,

26
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
which BurpScanner flags as issues.

27
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
To scan a single input, highlight the input.

28
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:27,000
You can do this from any message editor in Burp, for example from proxy HTTP history.

29
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Right-click and select Scan Selected Insertion Point.

30
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
In the Scan Launcher window, click OK to start the scan using the default configuration.

31
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,000
If you'd like to scan multiple inputs at the same time, send the request to BurpIntruder.

32
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,000
On the Intruder Positions tab, add a payload position for each input you want to scan.

33
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
You do this by selecting the input and clicking Add.

34
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Right-click the request and select Scan Defined Insertion Points.

35
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Again, when the Scan Launcher opens, click OK to start the scan using the default configuration.

36
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
BurpScanner will audit the request using only the selected inputs.

37
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:10,000
To view any issues identified by these scans, go to the Tasks panel on the dashboard,

38
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
click View Details beside the task to open the Task Details dialog,

39
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
and switch to the Issue Activity tab.

40
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
This will contain a record of all the issues found during the scan.

41
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Finally, we'll look at Fuzzing Input to identify potential vulnerabilities.

42
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Highlight the input in the request, right-click, and select Send to Intruder.

43
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Go to the Intruder Positions tab, and notice that because we highlighted it before sending it,

44
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
the input is automatically marked as a payload position.

45
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Now, go to the Intruder Payloads tab, and under Payload Settings, add a list of fuzz strings.

46
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:55,000
If you're using BurpSuite Professional, open the Add from List drop-down menu,

47
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,000
and select one of the built-in fuzzing lists.

48
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:04,000
For example, Fuzzing Full, or in this case, Fuzzing SQL Injection.

49
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Alternatively, you can add your own list.

50
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Click Start Attack.

51
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
The attack starts in a new dialog.

52
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
BurpIntruder will send a request for each Puz payload.

53
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,000
When the attack is finished, study the responses to look for any interesting behaviour,

54
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
such as this request that solves the lab.

